RunTime polymorphism:
Changing the behaviour of the method in runtime.
public class Program{
	public static void Main(string[] args){
		parent p1 = new child();
		p1.method();  		// output: child
	}
}
public class parent{
	public virtual void method(){
		Console.WriteLine("parent");
	}
}
public class child:parent{
	public override void method(){
		Console.WriteLine("child");
	}
}
=> Here parent variable is referred with child class. When we run, the child method will going to execute. This is the actual thing happens in run time polymorphism. Overriding is a concept that is the process of reimplementing method in child class but the run time polymorphism is different.
=> The advantage of run time polymorphism is decoupling.

