SOLID principles:
S -> Single responsibility principle
=> One class mush have only one responsibility. A class should have only one reason to change.

O -> Open/Closed Principle
=> Software entities should be open for extension but closed for modification. You should be able to add new functionality without changing existing code.
Bad Example:
class PaymentService {

    void pay(String type) {

        if(type.equals("CreditCard")) {
            // payment
        }

        if(type.equals("UPI")) {
            // payment
        }

    }

}

Good Example:
interface Payment {
    void pay();
}
class CreditCardPayment : Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("Credit Card");
    }
}
class UPIPayment : Payment {
    public void pay() {
        System.out.println("UPI");
    }
}
class MainProgram{
    public void main(){
	Payment payment = new UPIPayment();  	// Here based on the child class we are passing the method it will going to execute.
	payment.pay();
    }
}
=> If new payment option came then we just inherit the option and implement

L -> Liskov Substitution Principle (LSP):
=> Objects of a subclass should be replaceable with objects of the superclass without breaking the program. A child class should behave like its parent.

Bad Example:
class Bird {
    void fly() {}
}

class Penguin : Bird {
    void fly() {
        throw new UnsupportedOperationException();
    }
}

I -> Interface Segregation Principle (ISP):
=> To overcome the violation of Liskov Substitution Principle this principle came.
class Bird {
}
class FlyingBird : Bird {
    void fly() {}
}
class Sparrow : FlyingBird {
}
class Penguin : Bird {
}

D -> Dependency Inversion Principle:
=> High-level modules should not depend on low-level modules. The decencies doesn't relay on modules directly instead we will declare in constructor.
=> Dependency injection naturally support the Dependency Inversion Principle


Design Patterns:
It is like if you face this kind of situation we have to use this kind of pattern thats it.

Strategy pattern:
=> The Strategy Design Pattern is a "behavioral design pattern" that allows you to define a family of algorithms (strategies), place each one in a separate class, and make them interchangeable at runtime.

Ex:
public class Program
{
	public static void Main(string[] args)
	{
		PaymentStratigy  stratigy = new PaymentStratigy(new CardPayment());
		stratigy.DoPayment();							//output: CardPayment
		stratigy.SetObject(new CoupenPayment());
		stratigy.DoPayment();							//output: CoupenPayment
	}
}

public interface IPayment
{
	void MakePayment();
}

public class CardPayment:IPayment{
	public void MakePayment(){
		Console.WriteLine("CardPayment");
	}
}
public class CoupenPayment:IPayment{
	public void MakePayment(){
		Console.WriteLine("CoupenPayment");
	}
}

public class PaymentStratigy
{
	private IPayment PaymentObj;
	public PaymentStratigy(IPayment Payment){
		this.PaymentObj = Payment;
	}

	public void SetObject(IPayment Payment){	//Setter for the object
		this.PaymentObj = Payment;
	}

	public void DoPayment()
	{
		PaymentObj.MakePayment();
		SendInvoice();		//Extra method that happens after payment
	}
}

=> In the above example CardPayment, CoupenPayment are set of strategies and  PaymentStratigy is the context for the process, it is responsible for to group the strategies.
=> Here if any other payment option came means we just need to implement the IPayment interface and implement the logic and call in the main method.
=> Here the context class(PaymentStratigy) is responsible for flow execution this is the advantage of strategy pattern where we need a set of strategies to execute then we have to use it.
